From 981aea3d15b082d165ab06981667be735a05edb7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 03:54:48 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] board: samsung: exynos-mobile: add the Samsung Galaxy
 S10+

Brings the pieces of this port together: a configuration for the phone, the
device tree bits that only a boot loader wants, and an optional fragment
for the early console.

The storage of this phone is UFS, and neither its controller nor the clock
block that feeds it is in the shared device tree, because the kernel side
of this port has no driver for either yet. Both are described in the
board's own file, so the shared copy stays exactly what the kernel has and
the next sync of dts/upstream has nothing to merge. The window the PHY is
given is a page rather than the half page the vendor tree gives it, because
the calibration tables of that PHY reach past the half on the second lane;
that goes unnoticed in an operating system, which maps whole pages, and
would not here.

The configuration is the one this board already had for its Exynos7870
phones, with the three entries that name that SoC replaced and the drivers
of this one added. Two other things had to change. The number of memory
banks goes from three to six, because this phone has six and the parser
stops at the limit, so three of them would simply have been invisible. And
what is left out is USB, fastboot, eMMC and the power management chip:
none of them is on the path from reset to reading storage and handing over,
and none of them has been tried on this phone.

The early console is a fragment to merge by hand rather than part of the
configuration, because only one of its two numbers is known. The address is
this SoC's debug UART, taken from its device tree. The rate that block is
fed at has not been measured on this phone, and the value carried over from
the Exynos7870 phones is a placeholder the fragment says so about. The
console of the driver model does not need it, because it asks the clock
driver.

A page under doc/board/samsung tells the rest: that the device tree rides
inside the image here rather than being packed separately as it is for the
Exynos7870 boards of this family, what the boot image around it looks like,
and what to look for in the first log of one of these phones.

The screen comes up too, which is what makes one of these phones testable
without a serial cable. What runs before U-Boot leaves a picture on the
panel and tells the kernel it starts where that picture lives; this phone's
own boot loader passes s3cfb.bootloaderfb=0xca000000, which is where the
framebuffer node here points. The display controller only pushes that
picture out when it is triggered, though, and by the time U-Boot runs
nothing is triggering it, so the board hands it its software trigger. The
controller's address comes out of the device tree, so nothing happens on a
board whose tree has no such controller; the register offset and the value
are constants, and the value is the one uniLoader hands the sibling phone
of this family.

Both that trigger and a band of colour along the bottom of the screen go in
the earliest hook there is, before relocation, before any driver and before
the console, because on a phone there is nowhere else for a boot loader to
say that it got as far as running at all. Without the band, a boot loader
that started and then hung and one that never ran look exactly alike: the
panel keeps the picture it came up with either way. The band goes at the
bottom so the console, which starts at the top, can be read alongside it.
How far it paints is bounded by what the tree says is there rather than by
the geometry it also says, so a wrong stride cannot walk it off the end.

With a screen and the volume keys as a keyboard, the commands that make a
bring-up readable are worth having built in, so clk, dm, fdt and pinmux
come along: between them they answer whether a clock came out right and
whether a pin controller bound to the bank table it was meant to, which is
most of what goes wrong first.

None of the screen path has been tried on hardware, and the code says so.

Signed-off-by: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
