diff --git a/doc/board/samsung/exynos-mobile/exynos9820.rst b/doc/board/samsung/exynos-mobile/exynos9820.rst
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..9f804188
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/board/samsung/exynos-mobile/exynos9820.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,143 @@
+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0+
+.. sectionauthor:: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
+
+Samsung Exynos 9820 Boards
+==========================
+
+Overview
+--------
+
+These phones keep their storage on UFS rather than eMMC, so the block device
+U-Boot boots from appears as ``scsi 0`` and not ``mmc 0``. The controller, its
+PHY and the clock block that feeds them are all driven by U-Boot itself; the
+primary boot loader, S-BOOT, only loads and enters the image.
+
+Building
+--------
+
+If a cross-compiler is required, install it and set it up like so:
+
+.. prompt:: bash $
+
+	export CROSS_COMPILE=aarch64-linux-gnu-
+
+Then build. Unlike the other boards here, the device tree does not have to be
+named on the command line, because the configuration carries it:
+
+.. prompt:: bash $
+
+	make O=.output exynos9820-beyond2lte_defconfig
+	make O=.output -j$(nproc)
+
+If successful, the image is ``.output/u-boot.bin``.
+
+Preparation
+-----------
+
+The device tree is already inside that image: with ``CONFIG_OF_SEPARATE`` the
+build appends ``u-boot.dtb`` to ``u-boot-nodtb.bin``, and the two together are
+what ``u-boot.bin`` is. So unlike the Exynos 7870 boards, no separate device
+tree image has to be packed, and nothing has to be handed to ``mkbootimg``
+beyond the image itself.
+
+The image carries a Linux kernel image header, so S-BOOT loads it exactly as it
+would load a kernel. Pack it with ``mkbootimg``, using the boot image layout
+this phone's postmarketOS device package [1]_ describes. The one that comes with
+android-tools will do, and so will the one by osm0sis [2]_; unlike the
+Exynos7870 boards of this family, nothing here needs the latter's ``--dt``:
+
+.. prompt:: bash $
+
+	mkbootimg -o u-boot.img \
+		--kernel		.output/u-boot.bin \
+		--header_version	1 \
+		--pagesize		2048 \
+		--kernel_offset		0x00008000 \
+		--ramdisk_offset	0x01000000 \
+		--second_offset		0xf0000000 \
+		--tags_offset		0x00000100 \
+		--os_version		12.0.0 \
+		--os_patch_level	2021-12
+
+The base address is left at the tool's default, because that device package does
+not give one; with it the kernel lands at 0x10008000. S-BOOT on these phones
+also wants a trailer on the image, without which it refuses to boot it:
+
+.. prompt:: bash $
+
+	printf 'SEANDROIDENFORCE' >> u-boot.img
+
+Flashing
+--------
+
+The first time, flash from Samsung's Download (Odin) mode with Heimdall [3]_.
+Hold volume down and bixby while powering the phone on to get there:
+
+.. prompt:: bash $
+
+	heimdall flash --BOOT u-boot.img
+
+Once U-Boot is installed, its own fastboot interface can be used instead. Hold
+the volume down key to reach the boot menu, enable fastboot mode from there,
+then:
+
+.. prompt:: bash $
+
+	fastboot flash boot u-boot.img
+
+Note that fastboot on these phones can write the boot partition but not the
+storage behind it, because the option that names the partition to flash to
+speaks of eMMC and there is none.
+
+Bringing one up for the first time
+----------------------------------
+
+The screen works without any extra hardware. What ran before leaves a picture on
+the panel and tells the kernel it starts where that picture lives; the same
+address is in the device tree here, so U-Boot writes its own output there and
+the phone shows it. The display controller of this SoC only pushes the picture
+out when it is triggered, and nothing is triggering it by the time U-Boot runs,
+so the board hands it its software trigger before anything is drawn.
+
+That makes a phone with no serial cable enough to watch a boot: output on the
+panel, and the volume keys as the keyboard. ``clk dump`` prints the clock tree,
+``dm tree`` which drivers bound, and ``pinmux status`` the pin banks, which is
+usually enough to tell a wrong clock from a wrong pin without any instruments.
+
+Before any of that, the board paints a band of colour along the bottom of the
+screen, from the first hook there is: before relocation, before any driver and
+before the console. It is there to tell two failures apart that otherwise look
+identical, because the panel keeps the picture it came up with in both:
+
+=============================  ==================================================
+what the screen shows          what it means
+=============================  ==================================================
+band, then text above it       everything as far as the console works
+band, nothing above            U-Boot ran, the framebuffer address and the
+                               trigger are right, something after that hung
+the picture it booted with     U-Boot never ran, or the screen is not where the
+                               device tree says it is
+=============================  ==================================================
+
+So the band is the thing to look for first, not the text. Only the third row
+says nothing about the boot loader itself, and it is the one to start from: if
+the phone is otherwise alive, the trigger is the first thing to doubt, because
+everything else about the screen is read from the device tree and that one write
+is a constant.
+
+Two things are worth looking for in the first log. The UFS driver prints the
+rate of the UniPro clock, and everything the link is told about time is
+computed from it, so a wrong one gives a link that does not work rather than an
+error; ``clk dump`` shows where that rate comes from. And the link has to reach
+a high speed gear: the driver says so and stops if it did not, because the core
+skips the change of speed silently when it cannot work the best mode out.
+
+Reading a sector is the proof that storage works, not ``ufs init`` returning
+without an error: that command does not scan the SCSI bus, so a misconfigured
+PHY shows up later, at ``scsi scan``.
+
